@*
    Circle AI is two apps and only one of them has an icon.

    THE THIN APP CANNOT THINK. Every question travels app -> link -> the service,
    which is where the brain, the models, the voices and the skill library live.
    Installed on its own this app is a set of screens that cannot answer anything,
    and until now nothing on it said why - the link just reported that Circle AI was
    not installed, to somebody looking straight at the Circle AI they had installed.

    AND THE WAY OUT IS A FILE, NOT A LINK. A phone with nothing on it cannot reach a
    store or a mesh - fetching the brain over Aether would need AetherNetService,
    which is itself another install, which is the same problem one step along. What
    needs nothing at all is somebody who already has Circle AI handing over the
    installer through Android's own share sheet. Bluetooth is the floor.

    THE BLOCKING STATE ONLY, NOT THE FEATURE. Sending Circle AI to somebody is a
    thing a person DOES, so it lives in Services with the other things they can do -
    see SendApp. This is the app telling them it cannot work yet, which is neither a
    feature nor a setting and belongs in front of them.

    SILENT WHEN THERE IS NOTHING TO SAY. A complete phone renders no markup at all,
    so this can sit at the top of Home without becoming furniture.
*@
@inject IAppPairing Pairing

@if (!_facts.Complete)
{
    <section class="pairing" aria-labelledby="pairing-title">
        <h2 class="pairing-title" id="pairing-title">@Missing is not on this phone yet</h2>
        <p class="pairing-why">@AssistantPairingCopy.Why</p>
        <p class="pairing-how">@AssistantPairingCopy.HowToGet</p>

        @* THE PERMISSION IS A SETTINGS SCREEN, NOT A DIALOG. From Android 8 it
           cannot be asked for inline, so the honest thing is to say so and take
           them to their own switch rather than a list of every app on the phone. *@
        @if (!_facts.MayInstall)
        {
            <p class="pairing-gate">
                This phone also has to allow Circle AI to add it. That is one switch,
                in Android's own settings.
            </p>
            <button class="pairing-go" @onclick="OpenPermission">Open that setting</button>
        }
    </section>
}

@code {
    private PairingFacts _facts;

    /// <summary>What is missing, named the way the product names it.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ONE OWNER FOR THE WORDS. The same name the link uses when it refuses, so a
    /// person is not told about two different things that are the same thing.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Missing => AssistantPairingCopy.Name;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // ASKED, NOT REMEMBERED. Somebody can leave this screen, install the other
        // half, and come back - so the answer is read when the component appears
        // rather than cached for the life of the app.
        _facts = Pairing.Facts;
    }

    private void OpenPermission()
    {
        Pairing.OpenInstallPermission();
    }
}
